 "figures": {
  "heredity": {
   "title": "Is a child's brain like its parent's?",
   "caption": "Each dot is a world of round 3 that lived. Across: the heredity index as the births-only tree read it, playing a child against the agent that bore it. Up: the same index on the brain-copy tree, playing each brain against the brain it was copied from, by birth or by conquest. 1 means a child acts as its parent does, 0 as a stranger does.",
   "recipe": "**Runs.** every world of round 3 of the screen of random worlds that lived to the end of its run (Experiments 40, 41 and 42; 30,000 tokens, 3,000 iterations)\n\n**Data.** From each world's readings on its brain-copy tree, `GraphOfLifeRuns/.pilots/screen/braintree/<run>.json` (research/screen/braintree.py: one pass over every frame, births and conquests both copying brains, and 100 shadow worlds of pure luck; selection.py; brainplay.py, brains of the kept worlds played), gathered by `research/screen/evolution.py` into `research/screen/evolution.json`, with the births-only numbers of `research/screen/candidates.json` beside them.\n\n1. Read each world's `heredity_births_only` (`beside`) and its brain-tree heredity (`values`) from `evolution.json`.\n\n**To make it again:** `python3 book_figures.py evolutionrank`."
  },
  "ancestry": {
   "title": "How far back do the living meet one ancestor?",
   "caption": "Each dot is a world of round 3 that lived. Across: how many iterations before the end all its last agents' brains descend from one brain. Up: the same in 100 shadow worlds, whose copies are made at the same moments into the same living but from parents drawn at random (the median; a world whose shadows mostly never meet one ancestor within the run is drawn at 3,000). A dot above the line: the real ancestry narrows faster than luck would narrow it.",
   "recipe": "**Runs.** every world of round 3 of the screen of random worlds that lived to the end of its run (Experiments 40, 41 and 42; 30,000 tokens, 3,000 iterations)\n\n**Data.** From each world's readings on its brain-copy tree, `GraphOfLifeRuns/.pilots/screen/braintree/<run>.json` (research/screen/braintree.py: one pass over every frame, births and conquests both copying brains, and 100 shadow worlds of pure luck; selection.py; brainplay.py, brains of the kept worlds played), gathered by `research/screen/evolution.py` into `research/screen/evolution.json`, with the births-only numbers of `research/screen/candidates.json` beside them.\n\n1. Read each world's `common_ancestor_age` and `common_ancestor_age_shadow` (`beside`) from `evolution.json`.\n\n**To make it again:** `python3 book_figures.py evolutionrank`."
  },
  "novelty": {
   "title": "Does the line of descent find more than mutation alone?",
   "caption": "Each dot is a world of round 3 that lived. Across: how far the later eras of its line of descent, on the brain-copy tree, lie from every earlier era in the same situations, above the noise of playing one brain twice. Up: the same for six lines that start from the line's brain of iteration 250 and make as many changes as it made, at random. A dot below the line: the real line moved further than mutation alone would take it; above it, less far.",
   "recipe": "**Runs.** every world of round 3 of the screen of random worlds that lived to the end of its run (Experiments 40, 41 and 42; 30,000 tokens, 3,000 iterations)\n\n**Data.** From each world's readings on its brain-copy tree, `GraphOfLifeRuns/.pilots/screen/braintree/<run>.json` (research/screen/braintree.py: one pass over every frame, births and conquests both copying brains, and 100 shadow worlds of pure luck; selection.py; brainplay.py, brains of the kept worlds played), gathered by `research/screen/evolution.py` into `research/screen/evolution.json`, with the births-only numbers of `research/screen/candidates.json` beside them.\n\n1. Read each world's `novelty_above_noise` and `novelty_null_above_noise` (`beside`) from `evolution.json`.\n\n**To make it again:** `python3 book_figures.py evolutionrank`."
  }
